$[Import* std]
A last clause `{else body}` handles every alternative the other clauses do not name.
$[# {area s} {!case s {circle r {* 3.0 {* r r}}} {else 0.0}}]
${area {#circle 2.0}}
expect: 12.0
${area {#square 2.0}}
expect: 0.0
A function with an `else` accepts any variant that has the named alternatives.
$[# Shape [% {Variant {' {Field "circle" Float} {Field "rect" {Struct {' {Field "w" Float} {Field "h" Float}}}} {Field "dot" Unit}}}]]
${area {: {#rect {. w 1.0 h 2.0}} Shape}}
expect: 0.0
${!case {: {#dot unit} Shape} {dot u "dot"} {circle r "circle"} {else "other"}}
expect: "dot"
`else` alone handles everything, and the clause bodies may differ in effects only as usual.
${!case {#a 1} {else "anything"}}
expect: "anything"
${!case {some 3} {some x x} {else 0}}
expect: 3
${!case none {some x x} {else 0}}
expect: 0
The macro's binder for the value does not capture the user's names.
$[# _rest 7]
${!case {#b 1} {a x x} {else _rest}}
expect: 7
A variant may still have an alternative named `else` when it is not the last clause's keyword.
${!case {#else 5} {else x {+ x 1}}}
expect: 6
